# Normalize line endings in the repository to LF for every text file.
#
# 2026-08-16: added after a two-day debugging detour. server/server.js had two
# LITERAL NUL bytes in its source (`id + '\0' + name` written as raw 0x00
# instead of an escape sequence). Git's CRLF filter classifies any buffer
# containing NUL as binary and skips end-of-line normalization for it, so with
# core.autocrlf=true the CRLF working copy never got normalized back to the LF
# blob — `git diff server/server.js` reported all ~6,400 lines as changed when
# only ~260 actually were, which made two days of uncommitted work impossible
# to review before committing. ripgrep likewise refused to print matches from
# that file ("binary file matches"), silently hiding it from code search.
#
# Declaring `text=auto eol=lf` here makes normalization explicit and
# repository-wide instead of depending on each machine's core.autocrlf, so a
# stray byte can no longer quietly disable it for one file.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Generated replay scripts embed PowerShell helpers that MUST keep a UTF-8 BOM
# (powershell -File reads a BOM-less script as the system ANSI codepage, CP949
# here, which mangles Korean button names — CLAUDE.md §5). They are still text
# and still want LF; the BOM is content, not an encoding attribute, so the rule
# above is safe for them.

# Binary assets — never touch.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
